
When upgrading Sauron you may also need to update your database (format)
to same level (version) as the new version of Sauron requires.
Here is the current version history for database format:

Sauron version		database version
--------------		----------------
0.5.x			1.0
0.6.x			1.0
0.7.0			1.1
0.7.1			1.2
0.7.2			1.3
0.7.3			1.4
0.8.5			1.7
0.9.0			1.8


DATABASE BACKEND MIGRATION (Pg.pm → DBI/DBD::Pg)

Starting with Sauron 0.9.0, the default database backend has changed from
the legacy Pg.pm module to the modern DBI/DBD::Pg backend. The Pg.pm backend
is now DEPRECATED and should be migrated.

Benefits of DBI/DBD::Pg:
 - Better error handling and recovery
 - Improved performance with modern PostgreSQL versions
 - Better support for connection pooling and transactions
 - More consistent behavior across different PostgreSQL versions
 - Scheduled to be the only supported backend in Sauron 1.0

To migrate from DB-Pg.pm to DB-DBI.pm:

 Option A: Simple symlink replacement (if already installed)
   1) cd /usr/local/sauron/Sauron (or your installation directory)
   2) rm -f DB.pm
   3) ln -sf DB-DBI.pm DB.pm
   4) Test your installation: cd .. && ./status

 Option B: Rebuild from source with DBI backend (recommended)
   1) ./configure --with-DBI  (or just ./configure, DBI is default)
   2) make
   3) make install
   4) Test: cd /usr/local/sauron && ./status

 Option C: Remove and reinstall with proper backend
   1) make clean
   2) ./configure --with-DBI
   3) make
   4) make install

Known compatibility issues:
 - No known data loss issues when switching backends
 - Both backends use identical PostgreSQL database schema
 - Configuration strings remain the same (DB_DSN, DB_USER, etc.)
 - Performance should be similar or better with DBI backend

If you experience issues during migration:
 1) Check system logs for deprecation warnings
 2) Verify database connectivity: ./runsql "SELECT version();"
 3) Review Perl module dependencies (DBI, DBD::Pg)
 4) Consult documentation or file a bug report


UTF-8 WEB INPUT/OUTPUT COMPATIBILITY (0.9.0)

Sauron 0.9.0 now supports UTF-8 end-to-end in CGI/DBI path by default
(DBI with pg_enable_utf8 and UTF-8 request handling when configured charset
is UTF-8).

Compatibility behavior:
 - UTF-8 request handling is enabled only when configured CGI charset is UTF-8
   (SAURON_CHARSET/BROWSER_CHARSET =~ /utf-?8/i).
 - Legacy non-UTF-8 deployments keep previous behavior.

Recommended configuration for new/updated deployments:
 - SAURON_CHARSET = 'utf-8'
 - BROWSER_CHARSET = 'utf-8'

Detection helper (no DB changes):
 - dbcheck --check=encoding --verbose --yes
 - Optional custom character seed:
   dbcheck --check=encoding --verbose --yes --mojibake-chars='ž,š,ť'
   (you can also pass a plain string, e.g. --mojibake-chars='žšť')
 - Optional target charset used for mojibake generation/repair:
   dbcheck --check=encoding --verbose --yes --mojibake-charset='cp1250'

Optional assisted repair (interactive, requires --commit):
 - dbcheck --check=encoding --verbose --commit
 - You can review/edit found rows before applying updates.

If old data is already mojibake-corrupted (for example "Å¾luÅ¥ouÄ\x8DkÃ½"):
 - Run a one-time repair only for affected rows, then verify manually.
 - Example for zone comments (adjust WHERE to your dataset):

   UPDATE zones
      SET comment = convert_from(convert_to(comment,'LATIN1'),'UTF8')
    WHERE comment LIKE '%Ã%';

Always run a database backup before mass text repair.



TSIG KEY NAME VALIDATION (RFC8945, 0.9.0)

Sauron 0.9.0 enforces RFC8945-compliant TSIG key name validation. The new
valid_tsig_keyname() validator replaces the previous valid_texthandle() and
requires TSIG key names to follow DNS domain name rules:

Validation rules:
 - Allowed characters: alphanumeric (A-Z, a-z, 0-9), hyphens (-), 
   underscores (_), and dots (.)
 - Labels (parts between dots) must start with alphanumeric
 - Labels cannot end with hyphen
 - No consecutive dots
 - Trailing dot is allowed (FQDN notation, e.g. "key.example.com.")
 - Both relative names ("key1") and FQDNs ("key1.example.com") are accepted

BREAKING CHANGE:
If your database contains TSIG keys with names that violate these rules,
the keygen utility will reject them after upgrade. Specifically:

 - Names starting with underscore: "_key", "_transfer"
 - Names ending with hyphen: "key-", "transfer-"

Before upgrading, check for non-compliant key names:

  sudo -u postgres -- psql sauron -P pager=off -c \
    "SELECT id, name FROM keys WHERE type=1 AND protocol=2 AND (name ~ '^_' OR name ~ '-$');"

If any keys are returned, you have two options:

Option A: Rename the keys before upgrade
  sudo -u postgres -- psql sauron -P pager=off -c \
    "UPDATE keys SET name='renamed-key' WHERE id=<id>;"
  
  Then update any DNS server configurations that reference the old key name.

Option B: Temporarily relax validation (not recommended)
  Modify Sauron/Util.pm valid_tsig_keyname() to allow the problematic patterns,
  then plan a migration to compliant names.

Always backup your database before making changes to TSIG key names:

  pg_dump sauron > sauron-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d).sql



DATABASE FORMAT 1.7 -> 1.8 (0.9.0)

The conversion script sql/dbconvert_1.7to1.8 creates new tables (SSHFP,
TLSA, DS, NAPTR, CAA, catalog zones, approval workflow). PostgreSQL makes
the role that creates a table its owner, and the script intentionally does
not change table ownership (no ALTER TABLE ... OWNER TO). The script MUST
therefore be run as the database user Sauron connects as (DB_USER in the
config file, usually 'sauron'), which is also the owner of the existing
tables. Do NOT run it as the postgres superuser: the new tables would be
owned by postgres and Sauron would fail with "permission denied".

The script checks this at the start and aborts with an error such as

  invalid input syntax for type integer: "dbconvert_1.7to1.8 must be run
  as the owner of all tables, not as postgres (table owners: sauron)"

if the current user does not own all existing tables (or if public.zones
does not exist, i.e. the wrong database was selected). Run the script as a
single transaction that stops at the first error, so that a failed check
or statement leaves the database unchanged.

The DB_USER needs the CREATE privilege on schema public (granted when it
owns the database, see 'ALTER DATABASE sauron OWNER TO sauron' in README).

If your installation differs and the tables are owned by another role while
Sauron connects as DB_USER through GRANTs (e.g. init/full-privileges.sql),
run the script as the table owner (the check requires one owner of all
tables) and afterwards grant DB_USER access to the new tables and their
sequences, for example:

	GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public
	      TO sauron;
	GRANT USAGE, SELECT, UPDATE ON ALL SEQUENCES IN SCHEMA public
	      TO sauron;

 1) Make backup of your database:

	pg_dump sauron > sauron-backup-$(date +%Y%m%d).sql

 2) Check the owner of the existing tables (expected: one row, DB_USER):

	psql sauron -c "SELECT tableowner, count(*) FROM pg_tables
	                WHERE schemaname='public' GROUP BY 1;"

 3) Apply the script as DB_USER, using one of:

    a) runsql (recommended; uses the DB_DSN/DB_USER/DB_PASSWORD from the
       Sauron config file, runs in one transaction, stops on error):

	cd /usr/local/sauron
	./runsql sql/dbconvert_1.7to1.8

    b) psql connecting as DB_USER:

	psql -h localhost -U sauron -d sauron \
	     -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 --single-transaction \
	     -f sql/dbconvert_1.7to1.8

    c) psql as the postgres superuser, switching to DB_USER first:

	sudo -u postgres psql -d sauron \
	     -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 --single-transaction \
	     -c 'SET ROLE sauron' -f sql/dbconvert_1.7to1.8

 4) Verify that no table is owned by another user (expected: no rows):

	psql sauron -c "SELECT tablename, tableowner FROM pg_tables
	                WHERE schemaname='public' AND tableowner <> 'sauron';"

    If some tables were created by a wrong user (e.g. by an earlier run
    as postgres), fix them as the postgres superuser; sequences of SERIAL
    columns follow their table:

	ALTER TABLE <tablename> OWNER TO sauron;

 5) Check the database version (expected: 1.8):

	psql sauron -c "SELECT value FROM settings WHERE setting='dbversion';"



GENERAL UPGRADE PROCEDURE

 1) Make backup of your database!
 
 2) Identify which conversion script or scripts (if you're
    not upgrading from the previous version) you need to use.
    Conversion scripts are located in the sql/ directory.
    For example script for converting from 1.0 to 1.1
    would be named as: dbconvert_1.0to1.1.sql

 3) run upgrade scripts as the database user Sauron connects as
    (DB_USER, the owner of the existing tables), not as the postgres
    superuser, for example:

	cd /usr/local/sauron && ./runsql sql/<filename>

    or: psql -U <DB_USER> -v ON_ERROR_STOP=1 -1 -f <filename> sauron
    (see also DATABASE FORMAT 1.7 -> 1.8 above)

 4) vacuum database: psql sauron -c 'VACUUM ANALYZE;'
    (running 'VACUUM FULL' might be good idea, too)


